//! check: run(exit=trap(assert))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.trap.set, mem.str.repeat, std.str
//!
//! A negative repeat count traps `assert` — the builtin's own fault, on all
//! three lanes, and std does not guard it. s71 RULED the kind
//! (`[mem.str.repeat]`, wolf-lang#57): a negative count is a caller contract
//! violation, which is `assert`, where the runtime used to report `bounds`.
//!
//! This file is a CORRECTION held as a test, and it has now corrected the
//! same sentence twice. sc03 measured `"ab".repeat(-1)` as `""` under the
//! interpreter and `std.str.repeat`'s doc claimed that answer for five
//! sprints; sc09 measured `trap(bounds)` on every lane and wrote this file;
//! sc11's pin bump changed the KIND and this file went red inside a minute,
//! which is the whole argument for holding a claim about an implementation
//! in a test rather than in prose (F-0056, closed).

use std.str

fn main() -> !int {
    let out = str.repeat("ab", 0 - 1)
    if out == "" {} else { return 1 }
    0
}
